<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
    <string>en</string>
    <!-- What the home screen shows under the icon.

         S5LBox names the SILICON, which is what this project emulates, and the
         *Box suffix reads as "emulator" the way DOSBox and 86Box do. It also
         survives the roadmap: M-P2 adds a second machine profile on the
         S5L8950X, and a name tied to one OS version would go stale the moment
         that lands.

         CFBundleName follows PRODUCT_NAME, which is now S5LBox as well – the
         Xcode target, the .app, the .ipa and the entitlements file were all
         renamed once the CI workflow that pinned them could be edited. The
         only thing still called iOS3-VM is the working directory on disk,
         because 16 CMake build trees cache its absolute path and a folder name
         is not worth reconfiguring them for. -->
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>S5LBox</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>0.0.1</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>1</string>
    <!-- The app's Documents folder appears in Files under "On My iPhone",
         which is how a 239 MB IPSW gets in without going through a document
         picker: copy it into S5LBox's folder and the Firmware screen finds it
         where it already is.

         WHAT THIS EXPOSES, stated because it is not only the firmware. The
         same folder holds Machines/<id>/rootfs-work.img – a whole per-machine
         work image, around 445 MB each – and those become visible, movable
         and deletable. That is deliberate: a work image the user cannot copy
         off the device is a VM they cannot back up, and every emulator that
         ships on iOS makes the same trade.

         It also means the canonical firmware inputs can be overwritten by the
         user. That is already handled rather than newly risky: VMFirmwareBoot
         gates every one of the three on size AND SHA-256 before it is opened,
         so a replaced or truncated input is refused by name instead of being
         booted. The gate existed before this key did.

         LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace lets the importer read an archive
         where it sits rather than copying it into the container first, which
         on a 64 GB phone is the difference between needing 239 MB free and
         needing 478 MB. -->
    <key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
    <true/>
    <key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
    <true/>
    <key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
    <true/>
    <key>MinimumOSVersion</key>
    <string>13.0</string>
    <key>UILaunchScreen</key>
    <dict/>
    <key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
    <array>
        <string>arm64</string>
    </array>
    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
    </array>
    <key>UIStatusBarStyle</key>
    <string>UIStatusBarStyleLightContent</string>
</dict>
</plist>
